# Cloudflare Workers Static Assets — `_headers` file (Netlify-compatible).
#
# Vite copies this file verbatim out of `public/` into the client output
# directory during `pnpm run build`, where Cloudflare Workers Static
# Assets picks it up at deploy time and applies the listed response
# headers to every matching request. `pnpm exec vite preview` parses the
# same file and reports `Parsed N valid header rules`, so the rules below
# are observable locally as well as in production.
#
# Pattern syntax: Cloudflare's Static Assets uses Express-style named
# placeholders for path parameters, e.g. `/tools/:slug/app/*`. The
# Netlify-style `*` wildcard is restricted to one per rule, so we use
# `:slug` for the Tool slug and `*` only for the trailing path.
# Reference:
#   https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/headers/
#
# Scope limit (Issue #291): `_headers` governs STATIC ASSETS ONLY.
# Cloudflare documents that "Custom headers defined in the `_headers`
# file are not applied to responses generated by your Worker code, even
# if the request URL matches a rule." The `/` HTML is streamed by the
# TanStack Start SSR handler inside the Worker, so its Cache-Control is
# set in `src/server.ts` instead of here.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Security headers — Static Assets half of Issue #449
#
# Cloudflare documents that "Custom headers defined in the `_headers`
# file are not applied to responses generated by your Worker code", so
# the two surfaces cannot be served by one function and they must be
# kept in step by a check instead. The check is
# `scripts/check-security-headers.mjs`, which asserts every value below
# against `src/http/security-headers.json` (the canonical values the
# Worker emits for everything it generates itself) and against
# `delivery.iframe.allow` in the Tool manifest. Editing one side
# without the other fails `pnpm run validate:fast`.
#
# ONE VALUE PER HEADER, PER PATH — the load-bearing rule
# --------------------------------------------------------
# Measured against `pnpm preview` (the Cloudflare preview server reads
# this same file) — a local measurement, not a quoted platform
# guarantee; the file is written to be safe under either semantics:
#
#   GET /tools/prototype/app/index.html      # with /* + :slug rules both set
#   referrer-policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin, no-referrer
#   permissions-policy: <baseline>, <baseline>
#
# i.e. every MATCHING rule contributes, and the platform COMBINES the
# values instead of overriding. A combined value is not a valid
# `Referrer-Policy` (single token) and a duplicated
# `Permissions-Policy` key resolves by parser, not by policy. So this
# file is written so that no two rules that match the same path ever set
# the same header: `scripts/check-security-headers.mjs` enforces exactly
# that invariant, and a violation fails `validate:fast`. The one
# exception is `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'`, which
# every Tool rule states per Issue #448; identical CSP policies intersect
# to the same policy, so the check accepts identical duplicates and still
# rejects different values.
#
# Consequence for Permissions-Policy: the deny baseline is NOT on a
# wildcard. It sits on `/share/*` — the only host-owned static document
# surface — and the Tool namespace deliberately does not carry
# `microphone=()`. Chromium was measured to treat the CONTAINER
# document's header as the ceiling for a framed document:
#
#   shell `microphone=()` + <iframe allow="microphone"> + Tool document
#     granting the feature  ->  DENIED (NotAllowedError)
#   shell grant + <iframe allow="microphone">                    ->  GRANTED
#
# A blanket deny on the Tool namespace therefore breaks every Tool that
# legitimately needs the microphone, which is exactly the failure this
# Issue is meant to avoid. Which Tool may actually obtain it is decided
# by the host route's `<iframe allow=...>`, driven by the manifest.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/*
  # `nosniff` (OWASP ASVS V14.1.1) applies to every static byte: hashed
  # assets, `/share/**` documents and PDFs, favicon, Tool artefacts. It
  # is the only header a wildcard may own, because it has no per-path
  # variant and therefore never combines with a narrower rule.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff

/share/*
  # Host-owned static documents. These get the full deny baseline,
  # including `microphone=()`: nothing in the conference site asks for
  # a capture device. The `frame-ancestors` directive is the Issue #448
  # inbound embed policy for these documents.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'
  Permissions-Policy: bluetooth=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), geolocation=(), hid=(), microphone=(), payment=(), serial=(), usb=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CORS — Tool iframes
#
# Tool iframes are sandboxed `allow-scripts` only (no
# `allow-same-origin`). The iframe document therefore has an opaque
# origin, and any `<script type="module">` and `<link rel="stylesheet">`
# inside it becomes a CORS request — module scripts are always fetched
# with CORS, even without the `crossorigin` attribute. The default
# Static Assets response does not include `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`,
# so the browser blocks the bundle and React never mounts.
#
# We scope the wildcard CORS to `/tools/:slug/app/*` only — the
# namespacing guarantees these are same-origin Tool artefacts emitted
# by the Tool build orchestrator (scripts/build-tools.mjs). The host's
# own `/assets/*` keeps its default behaviour (same-origin module
# scripts from the host page, no CORS).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inbound embed policy — Tool artefacts (Issue #448)
#
# `frame-ancestors` answers "who may FRAME this document". That is a
# different question from the CORS question above ("who may READ it"),
# and a different question again from the host's OUTBOUND policy ("which
# Tools may this site embed, and under what sandbox" — owned by
# `src/tools/registry.ts` and the manifest's `delivery.iframe`). The
# three are deliberately not derived from each other.
#
# `'self'` is the value that keeps the existing Tool iframes working: the
# only ancestor of `/tools/<slug>/app/index.html` is our own shell at
# `/tools/<slug>`, same origin, so `'self'` permits it. A third party
# embedding a Tool document directly is denied. The shell itself is a
# Worker response, so it is covered by the sibling policy in
# `src/server.ts` (also `'self'` on public paths, `'none'` on Admin /
# Account) — nothing that works today stops working.
#
# The rule that matters is `/tools/:slug/app/*`, because the entry
# document is `<artifact_path>/index.html` and the `:slug/app` pattern
# matches only the bare path. Both rules carry the header so the whole
# Tool namespace is covered whichever one a future entry HTML lands on.
#
# Do NOT write `'none'` here: Tool documents are framed BY us, so `'none'`
# breaks every integrated Tool. (The `allow-scripts`-only sandbox gives the
# framed document an opaque origin, but `frame-ancestors` is matched
# against the ANCESTOR's origin, which is still the host.) Do NOT write
# `*`: that is precisely the third-party framing this issue denies.
# `scripts/check-frame-ancestors-policy.mjs` enforces both directions, and
# `test/integration/frame-ancestors.test.ts` proves the Worker half.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/tools/:slug/app/*
  # Issue #449: the Tool artefact's own `Referrer-Policy` (from
  # `delivery.iframe.referrer_policy`). No Permissions-Policy here on
  # purpose — see the ceiling note above.
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'

/tools/:slug/app
  # The iframe document itself is fetched as a navigation (not a
  # module fetch), so it does not need CORS — but a permissive
  # `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` is harmless and keeps the rule
  # comprehensive for the whole Tool namespace.
  Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-Tool permission grant (Issue #449)
#
# Permissions Policy needs BOTH halves for a feature to work: the host
# route renders `<iframe allow="microphone">` (container side, driven by
# `delivery.iframe.allow` in the Tool manifest) and the framed document
# must not deny the feature here (document side). This is the document
# half for the one registered Tool that declares a grant.
#
# The origin is the canonical production origin (ADR-0014). A `_headers`
# rule is static text, so it cannot derive the origin at request time
# the way the Worker half does; the grant is still origin-scoped, which
# is what keeps it off every other site that embeds the Tool.
#
# Merge semantics: a Cloudflare rule applies only to the paths it
# matches, and this file is written to be correct under either
# merge behaviour — every rule below sets a header no other matching
# rule sets (see ONE VALUE PER HEADER above), so combining and
# overriding produce the same bytes. The combination behaviour was
# measured on `pnpm preview`; if it ever changed, the failure mode is
# fail-closed — the Tool loses the microphone, it does not silently
# gain it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/tools/mic-level/app/*
  # Document half of the grant declared by `delivery.iframe.allow` in
  # the Tool manifest. Only Permissions-Policy is set here: CORS,
  # nosniff and the Tool `Referrer-Policy` come from the `:slug` rule
  # above, and repeating them would combine into an invalid value.
  #
  # The origin is the canonical production origin (ADR-0014). A
  # `_headers` rule is static text, so it cannot derive the origin at
  # request time the way the Worker half does; the grant is still
  # origin-scoped, which is what keeps it off any other site that
  # embeds the Tool.
  Permissions-Policy: bluetooth=(), camera=(), display-capture=(), geolocation=(), hid=(), microphone=(self "https://rebuildup.dev"), payment=(), serial=(), usb=(), xr-spatial-tracking=()
  Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Inbound embed policy — public static documents (Issue #448)
#
# `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'self'` for `/share/*` lives
# on that rule above, because ONE VALUE PER HEADER, PER PATH means the
# two #448 rules and the #449 header set must not re-declare each other.
#
# Static Assets answer BEFORE the Worker runs (`wrangler.jsonc` declares
# no `assets.run_worker_first` and no `not_found_handling`), so
# `src/server.ts` never observes these requests and the Worker's
# `'self'` default does not apply. Without a rule here such a document
# carried no `frame-ancestors` at all, which is the state Issue #448
# set out to end.
#
# `scripts/check-frame-ancestors-policy.mjs` discovers every
# `public/**/*.html` and fails when no rule gives that document a
# `frame-ancestors` directive, so a new `share/<slug>/` cannot ship
# unprotected. The gate is the only coverage available: the workerd pool
# declares no `assets` binding, so no runtime test can observe a Static
# Assets response header.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Caching — fingerprinted build assets (Issue #291)
#
# Baseline before this rule: Cloudflare Workers Static Assets serves
# every static file with `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`
# plus an `ETag`, so a repeat visit paid a full revalidation round trip
# for every JS/CSS chunk. Measured against `vite preview` (which applies
# this same `_headers` file):
#
#   GET /assets/index-B5Hb0lCH.css
#   cache-control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate   <-- before
#   etag: "7b5cc500ffc7c1439a50578c25494f28"
#
# Vite emits everything under `/assets/` with
# `build.rollupOptions.output.assetFileNames` at its default
# `assets/[name]-[hash][extname]`, so the bytes behind any given
# `/assets/*` URL are immutable for the lifetime of that deployment —
# a new build produces a new hash and therefore a new URL. One year of
# freshness with `immutable` is safe and removes the revalidation round
# trip entirely; `immutable` also tells the browser not to revalidate
# even on reload.
#
# Deliberately NOT made immutable:
#   /share/**            stable, unhashed URLs with mutable contents.
#   /tools/:slug/app/**  Tool bundles keep stable entry URLs (index.html).
#   Both keep the Static Assets default `max-age=0, must-revalidate`,
#   which is the correct policy for a file whose URL outlives its bytes.
#
# 31556952 s = 52 weeks, the value Cloudflare documents as its own
# recommendation for fingerprinted assets.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31556952, immutable

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Web App Manifest + icon family (Issue #504)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `/site.webmanifest` is referenced from every document head by
# `APP_ICON_LINKS` in `src/routes/__root.tsx`. The browser fetches it
# with a manifest request, and the Web App Manifest specification
# defines its media type as `application/manifest+json`; declaring it
# here removes the dependency on the `.webmanifest` extension mapping of
# whichever edge serves the file.
#
# Not declared `immutable`: the URL is stable and unhashed, so a future
# icon change must be visible to a client that already cached the
# manifest. `max-age=0, must-revalidate` is the Static Assets default
# and the correct policy for that shape.
#
# `/icons/*` keeps that same default. Spelled out rather than inherited
# only because these files are the ones an iOS home screen and an
# Android launcher cache on their own, often for weeks: a stale icon
# outlives the deployment that replaced it, so the policy must stay
# revalidating, never immutable.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/site.webmanifest
  Content-Type: application/manifest+json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
